      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine how far Jana walks in 10 minutes, we need to calculate the distance she covers in a smaller time period and then extrapolate the result. Here's a step-by-step approach to solving the problem:\n\n1. **Find the walking rate in miles per minute:**\n   - Jana walks 1 mile in 24 minutes. To find the distance she covers per minute, we divide the distance walked by the time taken.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the distance covered in 10 minutes:**\n   - We need to determine how far Jana walks in 10 minutes. We can use the walking rate from the previous step.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance in 10 minutes} = \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Time}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the values we have:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance in 10 minutes} = \\left(\\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\\right) \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance in 10 minutes} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the fraction and convert to a decimal:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{24} \\approx 0.4167 \\approx 0.4 \\text{ miles} \\quad (\\text{rounding to the nearest tenth})\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the distance Jana walks in 10 minutes is \\( \\boxed{0.4} \\) miles.",
